import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="About Anupam Kumar and this site: writing, cleaned datasets and browser-side tools on public finance and public policy."
>
  <article class="prose" data-pagefind-body>
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      I work on public finance and public policy, and I write about economics and
      geoeconomics more broadly. This site is where that writing lives, alongside
      the data behind it.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site is for</h2>

    <p>
      Its purpose is narrow and deliberate: <strong
        >to make economic information usable by people who are not economists</strong
      >. Public finance in India is not short of numbers. It is short of numbers
      anyone can find, check and reuse.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the site holds three things, in the order they matter. Writing, which is
      the point. Cleaned datasets, each carrying its source, the date it was
      retrieved, a description of every column with its unit, and a script that
      rebuilds it from the original files. And small tools that run entirely in
      your browser.
    </p>

    <h2>On the data</h2>

    <p>
      Anyone can post a spreadsheet. What makes a dataset worth citing is that it
      answers the four questions a careful reader always has: where exactly did
      this come from, what vintage is it, what are the units, and what was
      changed?
    </p>

    <p>
      Every dataset here answers all four. The original files are kept untouched
      alongside the cleaned ones, and the cleaning can be re-run from scratch by
      anyone who wants to check it.
    </p>

    <p>
      One thing in particular gets stated rather than assumed: Budget Estimates,
      Revised Estimates and Actuals for the same year are three different facts,
      not one figure corrected twice. Conflating them is the most common error in
      Indian fiscal datasets, and it quietly corrupts every comparison built on
      top.
    </p>

    <h2>On the tools</h2>

    <p>
      Everything computes in your browser. If you open a state finance
      spreadsheet in a tool here, that file never leaves your laptop, because
      there is no server for it to go to. For anyone in government, a think tank
      or a newsroom handling figures that are not yet public, that is not a
      technical detail.
    </p>

    <h2>On how this site is built</h2>

    <p>
      It is built to outlive its hosting. Everything of value — every essay,
      every dataset, every script — is a plain text file in
      <a href="https://github.com/asrshadow/anupamkumar.org">a public repository</a>,
      readable in any text editor, with no database and no service required to
      make sense of it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The hosting is assumed to be temporary and the domain is assumed to be
      permanent. If this site has to be rebuilt somewhere else in 2035, that is
      a clone and a build, not a recovery project.
    </p>

    <h2>Reuse and citation</h2>

    <p>
      The writing and the datasets are published under
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>, and
      the code under the MIT licence. Please reuse any of it, with credit.
    </p>

    <p>
      Official figures republished here keep their own publisher's licence, which
      is recorded with each dataset.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting in touch</h2>

    <p>
      Corrections are especially welcome, and on a site whose whole claim is
      provenance, a correction is a contribution rather than a complaint. Write
      to <a href="mailto:hello@anupamkumar.org">hello@anupamkumar.org</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
